theory:
## Guarding the Unsafe Boundary

```xiom
// C function signature: int strlen(const char* s)
extern "C" fn strlen(s: *Char) -> Int
  requires: s != null;
  ensures: result >= 0;

fn safe_strlen(s: Str) -> Int {
  return strlen(s.as_ptr());  // Contract checked before call
}
```

### Common FFI Contract Patterns
```xiom
// C: void* malloc(size_t size)
extern "C" fn malloc(size: Int) -> *Void
  requires: size > 0;
  ensures: result != null;

// C: void free(void* ptr)
extern "C" fn free(ptr: *Void)
  requires: ptr != null;

// C: int memcmp(const void* s1, const void* s2, size_t n)
extern "C" fn memcmp(a: *Void, b: *Void, n: Int) -> Int
  requires: a != null;
  requires: b != null;
  requires: n > 0;
```

### Array + Length Patterns
```xiom
extern "C" fn qsort(base: *Void, count: Int, size: Int, cmp: *fn(*Void, *Void) -> Int)
  requires: base != null;
  requires: count > 0;
  requires: size > 0;
  requires: cmp != null;
```

### Why FFI Contracts Matter
Without contracts: `strlen(null_ptr)` crashes deep in C runtime.
With contracts: traps at the XIOM boundary with a clear message.

